(window.LADDER_CARDS["41a"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41a"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41a1.1","q":"What are the parts of a tape machine, and what is a configuration? What does the one-next technique say, and why is a run of a tape machine one of 4l’s runs?","a":"A finite set of marks with the blank, a finite set of states with a start and the stop states, a table δ giving for each other state and mark a mark to write, a step L or R and a next state, and a tape endless to the right; a configuration is the state, the tape and the cell the head is on; a configuration whose state is not a stop state has exactly one next, found by looking at one cell; the configuration is 4l’s state, the stop test looks at the state, the move is one row of the table and the output is the tape at the stop","w":["An endless set of marks and states and a tape; a configuration is the table; each configuration has several next ones, and the machine chooses; a run is not one of 4l’s runs, since it has no stop test","A finite set of marks and states, a table and a tape; a configuration is the mark under the head; the one-next technique says each mark is read once; a run is 4l’s run because the tape is endless","A tape and a head with no table; a configuration is the input; the one-next technique says the head always steps right; a run is 4l’s run because it always stops"],"why":"δ is a function (4d2): one value for each pair of state and mark."},
 {"u":"41a2.1","q":"What does it mean for a machine to compute a function, to decide a set, and to list a set? What does the both-sides technique say, and why does running two machines in turns stop?","a":"It computes f when, started on any w, it stops with f(w) on the tape; it decides A when it stops on every input, in yes exactly on the members of A; it lists A when it stops in yes exactly on the members, and on other strings may stop in no or never stop; a set is decided exactly when it and the strings outside it are both listed; w is in A or outside it, so one of the two listers stops in yes","w":["To compute is to stop on some input; to decide and to list are the same; a set is decided when it is listed; the turns stop because both machines always stop","To decide A is to stop in yes on the members and never stop on the rest; to list A is to stop on every input; a set is listed exactly when it is decided; the turns stop after a fixed number of steps","To compute f is to write f(w) without stopping; a set is decided when either it or its outside is listed; the turns stop because the first machine is run to its end before the second starts"],"why":"The proof does not say how long to wait, only that the wait ends."},
 {"u":"41a3.1","q":"What does the tracks technique say? Why does one old step cost at most about 2(n + t) new steps, and why does a power of n stay a power of n?","a":"For every machine with k tapes there is a machine with one tape that computes the same function, and t steps on an input of length n become at most about t(n + t); after t old steps no head is past cell n + t, and one old step is two sweeps over the used stretch; the slowdown squares the count, so n² becomes about n⁴, still a power of n","w":["A machine with k tapes computes more than any machine with one; one old step costs k new steps; a power of n becomes 2ⁿ","For every machine with k tapes there is one with one tape taking the same number of steps; no step costs more, so every count stays as it was","For every machine with k tapes there is one with one tape taking about t(n + t) steps; one old step costs 2(n + t) because the tape is copied k times; a power of n stays a power only for k = 2"],"why":"More tapes, no more reach; 41h leans on this."},
 {"u":"41a4.1","q":"What does the thesis claim, and why can it not be proved as a technique? What two kinds of evidence hold it up, and what is lost from 41d without it?","a":"Whatever a person can compute by following a finite list of techniques, with pencil and paper and no insight, some tape machine computes; one side of it, following a list of techniques, was never made exact; the evidence is an analysis of a person computing, in bounded steps that are a few rows of a table, and 41b’s finding that every other exact try reaches the same functions; without it 41d still proves that no tape machine decides which tape machines stop, but not that no recipe at all does","w":["Every function is computed by some tape machine; it cannot be proved because there are too many functions; the evidence is 41c’s count; without it 41d’s proof fails as mathematics","Whatever a tape machine computes, a person can compute; it is proved by 41b; no evidence is needed; nothing is lost from 41d without it","Whatever a person can compute, some tape machine computes; it cannot be proved because people make mistakes; the evidence is that machines are faster; without it 41d has no machine to run"],"why":"Lock the loose word before proving anything about it, and say which side of the lock each claim is on."},
 {"u":"41a5.1","q":"Why does a long run of a lister on w not show that w is outside the set? Why does 41b not prove the thesis?","a":"A lister that has not stopped has said nothing, and it may stop at the next step; and 41b proves exact machines equal to each other, while the thesis is about the loose word, which no proof touches","w":["After n² steps a lister must have stopped on every member; and 41b proves the thesis for built functions","A long run shows that w is probably outside; and 41b does prove the thesis, by the equal-reach technique","A lister stops on every input, so a long run cannot happen; and 41b is about a different thesis"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41b"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41b"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41b1.1","q":"What are the start functions, and what are the three ways of making new functions? What does the built-runs technique say, and which of the three ways can make a machine that never stops?","a":"Zero, Z(x) = 0, next, S(x) = x + 1, and the picks, Pᵢ(x₁, …, xₖ) = xᵢ; putting inside, recursion and search, the least y with f(x, y) = 0; every built function is computed by a tape machine; search can fail to stop: if no such y comes, the machine tries y = 0, 1, 2, … for ever and h has no value there","w":["Adding and multiplying; putting inside, recursion and search; every tape machine is built; recursion can fail to stop, since it calls itself","Zero, next and the picks; putting inside and recursion only; every built function has a value at every input; none of the ways can fail to stop","Zero, next and the picks; putting inside, recursion and search; every built function is computed by a tape machine; putting inside can fail to stop, since it runs several machines"],"why":"Search is the loop whose count is not known before it starts."},
 {"u":"41b2.1","q":"What does the one-search technique say? Why is next built without search, and where does the one search go?","a":"For every tape machine with code e there are functions next, start and out, built without search, such that the output on x is out(run(e, x, y)), where y is the least number of steps after which the state is a stop state; next reads the mark under the head and the state and looks up the row, by remainders and quotients by powers of |Γ| with cases; the one search is for that least y, with halted(e, run(e, x, y)) = 0","w":["Every tape machine is built without any search; next is built by search for the right row; no search is left","Every tape machine needs one search for each loop of its table; next is built without search; the searches go where the loops were","Every tape machine with code e has its output given by out(run(e, x, y)) for every y; next needs a search for the head; the one search is for the input x"],"why":"However many searches a building used, one is enough."},
 {"u":"41b3.1","q":"What does the search-graph technique say? Which flag of 38h does it close, and why is it enough that Proof and its denial are both search formulas?","a":"If a tape machine computes f, then “f(x) = z” is a search formula F(x, z), and when f(n) = m, PA reaches F(n̄, m̄) and ∀z (F(n̄, z) ⇒ z = m̄); it closes what 38h1 and 38h2 held in trust, that Proof and Sub are formulas of arithmetic of the right shape; Proof is decided by 38b3’s check, so by the both-sides technique it and its denial are both listed, and whichever is true at given numbers, PA reaches it","w":["If a tape machine computes f, PA reaches every true statement about f; it closes 38i; it is enough because every formula is a search formula","If f is built, “f(x) = z” is a bounded formula with no search; it closes 38g3; the denial of Proof is not needed","If a tape machine computes f, then “f(x) = z” is a search formula; it closes 38h1; it is enough because PA reaches the denial of every false search formula"],"why":"That is all 38h1, 38h2 and 38i used."},
 {"u":"41b4.1","q":"What are the terms of the calculus of functions, and what is its one move? What does the numeral-iterator technique say, and what is held in trust in the equal-reach technique?","a":"Placeholders, λx.M, the function that sends x to M, and (M N), M fed N; the one move is feeding: (λx.M) N may be rewritten as M with N put in for x, renaming bound placeholders so that none is caught; the term λn.λf.λx. f(n f x) turns the numeral of n into the numeral of n + 1 by feeding alone; held in trust is that the leftmost order finds the finished term whenever any order does","w":["Numbers and functions; the moves are adding and feeding; the numeral-iterator adds two numerals; held in trust is that every term has a finished form","Placeholders, λx.M and (M N); the one move is renaming; the numeral of n is the number n itself; nothing is held in trust","Placeholders, λx.M and (M N); the one move is feeding; the term λn.λf.λx. n f x turns n̄ into the numeral of n + 1; held in trust is that feeding can be done by a machine"],"why":"The numeral of n is the term that does a function n times."},
 {"u":"41b5.1","q":"Why may a search have no value? Why must the bound y be renamed before y is fed to λx.λy. x?","a":"There may be no y with f(x, y) = 0; then the search runs for ever and h has no value at x; and without renaming the fed y is caught by the inner λy, giving λy. y in place of λz. y","w":["A search always has a value, since the whole numbers have a least member; and renaming is only for tidiness","A search has no value when f is built without search; and y must be renamed because a term may not use a letter twice","A search has no value when two y give 0; and the bound y is renamed so that x is caught"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41c"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41c"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41c1.1","q":"How is a row of a table written as a string, and why can the code be read back in one way only? What does the table-code technique say, and how does it list the machines?","a":"A row δ(q, s) = (s′, d, q′) is five blocks of 0s, q, s, s′, d, q′ of them, with a single 1 between neighbours, and rows are joined by 11; a block of 0s never holds a 1, so the split is in one way only; every tape machine has a code, different tables have different codes, and whether a string is a code is decided; list the strings of 0s and 1s by length and then in order, and sift for codes: M₀, M₁, M₂, …","w":["A row is five numbers in base ten; it reads back in one way because each number has one digit; every string is the code of a machine; the machines are listed by size of tape","A row is five blocks of 1s with a 0 between; it can be read back in several ways; the technique says codes are listed but not decided; the machines are listed by running them","A row is five blocks of 0s with single 1s between; it reads back in one way; the technique says different tables may share a code; the machines are listed by their number of states only"],"why":"δ(1, 1) = (2, 2, 2) is written 0 1 0 1 00 1 00 1 00."},
 {"u":"41c2.1","q":"What does U keep on each of its three tapes? What does the universal technique say, and why is one step of M a bounded number of steps of U?","a":"⟨M⟩, which never changes; M’s tape, each mark as its block of 0s; and M’s state as a block of 0s; U on ⟨M⟩ # w stops exactly when M on w stops, in the same stop state and with the same output; a step of M is finding the row on tape 1 and copying its three other blocks, a bound that grows with the length of ⟨M⟩ and of the cell, never with t","w":["Its own table, its input and its output; U stops on every input; a step of M is one step of U","⟨M⟩, M’s tape and M’s state; U on ⟨M⟩ # w stops exactly when M stops on every input; a step of M costs more of U’s steps the longer M has run","⟨M⟩ on all three, for safety; U gives the same output as M whenever U has more states than M; a step of M is bounded because M’s tape is finite"],"why":"U’s table is fixed and finite: the stored program."},
 {"u":"41c3.1","q":"What does the few-machines technique say? Which technique of rung 35 does its proof run, and why does the proof not name the missed question?","a":"Some yes-or-no question about numbers is answered by no tape machine; the machines can be listed, so the sets they decide can be listed, and by the parts-outrun technique (35c) the sets of numbers cannot; the proof is a count: it names no machine and no question, and which question is missed depends on running each machine on its own number","w":["Every yes-or-no question about numbers is answered by some machine; 35a’s listing of pairs; the proof names each question in turn","Some question is answered by no machine; 35c’s parts-outrun technique; the proof names the halting question as the missed one","Some machine answers no question; 35b; the proof does not name it because machines have no names"],"why":"The questions machines answer are a countable crowd among uncountably many."},
 {"u":"41c4.1","q":"Why does U not need more states than the machines it runs? Why does the few-machines technique not show which question has no machine?","a":"U’s table is fixed, and it runs machines with any number of states, written on its tape; and the count names no question: 41d proves the halting one","w":["U grows a state for each state of M; and the few-machines technique shows that the halting question has no machine","U needs one more state than M; and the technique lists the questions with no machine in order","U has no states at all; and the technique shows that every question has a machine but one"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41d"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41d"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41d1.1","q":"What is the stopping set H? What does the stopping-listed technique say, and what does its machine do on a string outside H?","a":"H is the set of strings ⟨M⟩ # w such that M, started on w, stops; a machine lists it: check that the input is ⟨M⟩ # w, run U on it, and stop in yes if U stops; on a string that is no such form it stops in no, and on a pair where M never stops it runs for ever, which a lister may","w":["H is the set of machines that stop on every input; a machine decides it by running U; outside H it stops in no","H is the set of strings ⟨M⟩ # w such that M never stops on w; a machine lists it by waiting; outside H it stops in yes","H is the set of codes of machines; the technique says H is decided by 41c1’s check; outside H the machine stops in no"],"why":"While U runs, nothing tells whether it is nearly done or never will be."},
 {"u":"41d2.1","q":"What does the no-stop-decider technique say? What does E do, and why does running E on its own code refute D?","a":"No tape machine decides the stopping set; E, on ⟨M⟩, runs the supposed decider D on ⟨M⟩ # ⟨M⟩ and does the opposite of what D predicts: it loops for ever if D says yes and stops if D says no; then E stops on ⟨E⟩ exactly when D says it does not, which is exactly when it does not stop: ⊥, so there is no such D","w":["No machine lists the stopping set; E runs D on every input in turn; E on ⟨E⟩ takes too many steps for D to follow","No tape machine decides the stopping set; E does what D predicts; E on ⟨E⟩ stops and does not stop in turn, as a clock, so D oscillates","Some machines decide the stopping set, but slowly; E is a faster decider; running E on ⟨E⟩ shows that D is slower than E"],"why":"It is 35b’s unlisted real, with “stops” for a digit."},
 {"u":"41d3.1","q":"What does the not-stopping technique say, and which two techniques does its proof join? Why is “M stops on w within t steps” decided?","a":"No machine lists the strings ⟨M⟩ # w such that M never stops on w; if one did, H and the strings outside H would both be listed, so by the both-sides technique (41a2) H would be decided, against the no-stop-decider technique; with a limit t the question is decided by running U for t steps","w":["No machine decides the never-stopping strings, though one lists them; it joins the tracks and universal techniques; with a limit t the question is listed, not decided","No machine lists the never-stopping strings; it joins the table-code and few-machines techniques; “within t steps” is decided because every machine stops within t steps","Some machine lists the never-stopping strings; it joins the both-sides and stopping-listed techniques; “within t steps” is decided by asking D"],"why":"The question that cannot be decided is the one with no t attached."},
 {"u":"41d4.1","q":"Why does the no-stop-decider technique not say that some pair M, w can never be settled by anyone? Why is H a set that is listed and not decided?","a":"The technique says no single machine is right on every pair; each pair alone may be settled by some argument; and H is listed by the stopping-listed technique and not decided by the no-stop-decider technique, so a set that is not decided may still be listed","w":["The technique names one pair that nobody can settle; and H is neither listed nor decided","The technique fails for fast machines, which settle every pair; and H is decided but not listed","Every pair is settled by running U long enough; and H is listed because it is decided"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41e"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41e"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41e1.1","q":"When does a set A reduce to a set B? What does the reduction-carries technique say, and how does M_w turn the stopping question into the blank-tape question?","a":"When some machine computes a function f with x in A exactly when f(x) is in B; if A reduces to B and B is decided then A is decided, so if A is not decided, B is not; M_w writes w on the tape, walks back to the first cell and then acts as M, so M_w on a blank tape does what M does on w, and H reduces to the blank-tape question","w":["When A is a part of B; if A reduces to B and A is decided then B is decided; M_w erases w and runs M on a blank tape","When some machine decides both; if A reduces to B then both are decided or neither; M_w runs M for |w| steps","When some machine computes f with x in A exactly when f(x) is in B; if A reduces to B and A is decided then B is decided; M_w turns the blank-tape question into the stopping question"],"why":"f turns every case of A into a case of B with the same answer."},
 {"u":"41e2.1","q":"What makes a property about what machines do, and when is it trivial? What does the behaviour technique say, and why is “the table has five rows” not covered by it?","a":"Two machines computing the same function both have it or neither does; it is trivial when every machine’s function has it, or none does; for every property about what machines do that is not trivial, no machine decides which codes ⟨M⟩ have it; the number of rows is about the text of a program, which can be read, and is decided by counting","w":["It is about the machine’s table; it is trivial when it is easy to check; the technique says every property is undecided, the five rows included","Two machines with the same code both have it; it is trivial when one machine has it; the technique says such properties are decided by running the machine","It is about what machines do when every machine has it; it is never trivial; five rows is not covered because five is too small"],"why":"H reduces to P by N_{M,w}: first run M on w, then run M₁ on x."},
 {"u":"41e3.1","q":"What does the reach-undecided technique say? Which two facts about PA make σ reached exactly when M stops on w?","a":"No machine decides which statements PA reaches; σ says that the run of M on w has stopped by some step y, a search statement; if M stops, σ is true and PA reaches every true search statement (38g4); if M never stops, σ is false in ℕ and PA reaches only statements true in ℕ (38g2); so H reduces to what PA reaches","w":["No machine lists what PA reaches; PA reaches every true statement, and no false one","Some machine decides which statements PA reaches; PA reaches σ whenever M has a code, and never otherwise","No machine decides which statements PA reaches; PA reaches every statement or its denial, and both for σ"],"why":"38b listed what PA reaches; the list cannot be made into a decider."},
 {"u":"41e4.1","q":"What does the equation-search technique say? What did the technique of 1970 add to it, and how does that give an equation question no machine decides?","a":"The polynomial equations with integer coefficients that have a whole-number solution are listed: try the lists of whole numbers in order and stop in yes at the first zero; the 1970 technique adds that every listed set A of numbers has a polynomial p with a in A exactly when p(a, x₁, …, xₖ) = 0 has a whole-number solution; take A to be the codes of stopping pairs, and the stopping question reduces to solvability","w":["The solvable equations are decided by trying numbers up to the largest coefficient; the 1970 technique added a faster search; the question is decided","The equations with no solution are listed; the 1970 technique showed that x² + y² = 3 has none; so no single equation’s question is decided","The solvable equations are listed; the 1970 technique showed that every equation has a certificate when it has no solution; so the unsolvable ones are listed too"],"why":"The ladder names the 1970 technique and does not prove it."},
 {"u":"41e5.1","q":"Why does a decided A that reduces to B say nothing about B? Why does the behaviour technique say nothing about the number of rows of a table?","a":"That is backwards: f carries a decider of B to one of A, never the other way; and the number of rows is about the text, not what the machine does: count the rows","w":["A decided A makes B decided, by running f backwards; and the number of rows is undecided, by the behaviour technique","A and B are always decided together; and the rows cannot be counted without running the machine","f carries a decider of A to one of B; and the behaviour technique covers the rows only for machines that stop"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41f"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41f"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41f1.1","q":"What do the two halves A and B of SELF each do? What does the self-print technique say, and why is A built after B and not before?","a":"A writes B’s code and stops; B reads the tape, ⟨X⟩, computes q(⟨X⟩), the code of the printer of ⟨X⟩, and writes it in front; some machine, started on a blank tape, stops with its own code on the tape; A is P_⟨B⟩, the printer of B’s code, so B must be there first for A to be built from it","w":["A writes the code of SELF and B erases it; some machine prints the code of every machine; A is built first because it runs first","A writes its own code and B writes its own; some machine stops with its own code on the tape; A and B are built at once, each holding the other","A reads the tape and B writes ⟨A⟩⟨A⟩; no machine can print its own code; A is built after B because B is shorter"],"why":"The tape reads ⟨A⟩⟨B⟩ = ⟨SELF⟩; rung 0’s quotation trick is the same."},
 {"u":"41f2.1","q":"What does the own-code technique say? How does its proof match the self-statement technique of 38h2?","a":"For every machine T that reads a code and a string, there is a machine R such that R on any w does what T does on ⟨R⟩ # w; 38h2 put a formula’s code into the formula, by Sub, and here the code is put into the machine, by q: both describe, then feed the description to itself","w":["For every machine T there is a machine R that decides whether T stops; it matches 38h2 because both use induction","Every machine holds a copy of its own code in its table; it matches 38h2 because a formula holds its own code too","For every T there is an R with R on w doing what T does on w; it matches 38h2 only in its name"],"why":"R = A B T, three parts run in turn."},
 {"u":"41f3.1","q":"How does the own-code technique shorten the proof of 41d2? What does the shortest-unlisted technique say, and why does a C longer than R always come?","a":"R gets ⟨R⟩, runs the supposed D on ⟨R⟩ # w and does the opposite, so R stops on w exactly when D says it does not: ⊥ in three lines; no machine lists endlessly many shortest machines; only finitely many codes are as short as ⟨R⟩, so an endless list must hold a longer C","w":["It lets D read its own code; the technique says there are no shortest machines; a longer C comes because L lists every machine","It removes the need for D; the technique says a machine lists all the shortest machines; C comes because R writes it","It shortens nothing; the technique says only finitely many shortest machines exist; C comes because codes grow with time"],"why":"R acts as C with a shorter code, so C is not shortest, against L’s list."},
 {"u":"41f4.1","q":"Why can SELF not hold a copy of its own code? Why does the shortest-unlisted technique not say there are no shortest machines?","a":"⟨R⟩ is longer than any table holding it: A holds B’s code only, and B makes A’s code from it; and endlessly many shortest machines exist, one for each computed function at least, but no machine lists endlessly many of them","w":["SELF does hold a copy of its own code; and the technique says no shortest machine exists","A code cannot be written on a tape; and shortest machines exist but cannot be run","SELF holds half of its code twice; and the technique says only one shortest machine exists"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41g"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41g"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41g1.1","q":"What is a pattern, and which tape machines are machines with no memory? What does the joined-machines technique say, and why is “do these two machines check the same set?” decided for them?","a":"A pattern is a set of strings checked by a machine with no memory: a tape machine that never writes, always steps right and stops at the first blank; if such machines check A and B, then such machines check A and B, A or B, and the strings not in A; the set where two machines differ is then a pattern, and whether a pattern machine accepts any string is decided by looking for an accepting state the start can reach","w":["A pattern is any listed set; machines with no memory are those with one state; the technique gives and but not or; the sameness question is undecided, by 41e","A pattern is a set checked by a machine with a stack; machines with no memory never stop; the technique says patterns are closed under not only; sameness is decided by running both on every string","A pattern is a set checked by a machine with no memory; the technique says the join of two patterns needs a tape; sameness is decided because both machines have the same states"],"why":"The pair machine has p times q states."},
 {"u":"41g2.1","q":"What does the pumping technique say, and where does its proof use pigeonhole? Why does the string of q² a’s show that the strings of square length are no pattern?","a":"If a machine with no memory with q states checks L, every member w with at least q letters can be cut as w = xyz, y not empty and xy at most q letters, with xyⁱz in L for every i ≥ 0; the q + 1 states after 0, 1, …, q letters cannot all differ, so two are the same and y is a loop; for q² a’s, y is k a’s with 1 ≤ k ≤ q, and q² < q² + k < (q + 1)², so xy²z is not of square length","w":["Every string of a pattern can be cut anywhere and repeated; pigeonhole is used on the letters; q² a’s pump to q² + q², which is a square","If a machine with q states checks L, every member with at least q letters has xyⁱz in L for i ≥ 1 only; pigeonhole picks the longest loop; q² a’s cannot be cut at all","Every set that pumps is a pattern; pigeonhole counts the accepting states; q² a’s show the squares pump, so they are a pattern"],"why":"The loop is run i times, then z as before."},
 {"u":"41g3.1","q":"What is a stack machine, and how does it check balanced brackets? What does the two-loop technique say, and why must some placeholder appear twice on a path?","a":"A machine with no memory that also keeps a pile of marks it may add to or take from at the top only; it pushes at each opening bracket, pops at each closing one, fails if the stack is empty when a pop is needed and accepts when it is empty at the end; with p placeholders and right sides no longer than b, every made string longer than bᵖ can be cut as uvxyz, v and y not both empty, with uvⁱxyⁱz made for every i ≥ 0; a tree with more than bᵖ letters at its foot has a path with more than p placeholders, so by pigeonhole one repeats","w":["A tape machine with two heads; it counts brackets on the tape; the technique says every long string has one loop xyⁱz; a placeholder repeats because techniques are reused in order","A machine with no memory and a pile; it pushes at every bracket; the technique says uvⁱxyⁱz is made for i = 1 only; a placeholder repeats because the string has more than p letters","A machine with a pile; it pops at each opening bracket; the technique says strings shorter than bᵖ can be pumped; a placeholder never repeats on a path"],"why":"So aⁿbⁿcⁿ is in no grammar of the second family."},
 {"u":"41g4.1","q":"What does the bounded-room technique say, and why does a run longer than q · r · mʳ steps never stop? Which example separates each family from the one below it?","a":"If a tape machine never uses more than r cells, whether it stops is decided: it stops within q · r · mʳ steps, or never; there are at most q · r · mʳ configurations, so a longer run repeats one, and from a repeated configuration the run repeats for ever, by the one-next technique; aⁿbⁿ is in the second family and no pattern, aⁿbⁿcⁿ in the third and not the second, and the stopping set in the fourth and not decided","w":["A machine in r cells stops within r steps; a longer run has left its room; aⁿbⁿ separates the third family from the second","A machine in r cells may run for ever without repeating; so stopping is not decided; no example separates the families","A machine in r cells stops within q · r · mʳ steps or never, since it runs out of marks; aⁿbⁿcⁿ is a pattern, and aⁿbⁿ is not"],"why":"The refusals of 41d live only at the top of this ladder of machines."},
 {"u":"41g5.1","q":"Why may the proof that a set is no pattern not choose the cut xyz? Why is a machine with two stacks as strong as a tape machine?","a":"The machine chooses the cut, so the proof must defeat every cut with xy at most q long; and two stacks are a tape, one on each side of the head","w":["The proof may choose the cut that suits it; and two stacks are weaker than a tape, since each is read at the top only","The cut is fixed by the first letter; and two stacks are as strong as one","The cut must have y empty; and two stacks are a tape only when both are empty"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41h"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41h"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41h1.1","q":"When is a set fast, and what is the class P? What does the power-stays technique say, and why does it make P the same for every machine?","a":"A set is fast when some machine decides it within some power of n, nᵏ steps for a fixed k, and the fast sets form P; whether a set is fast does not depend on how many tapes the machine has, and a fast machine whose output is fed to a fast machine makes a fast machine; k tapes within nᵃ become one tape within about 2n^(2a), and nᵃ then mᵇ is at most n^(ab): a power of n, either way","w":["A set is fast when a machine decides it within n steps; P is the set of fast machines; the technique says more tapes make a set fast","A set is fast when some machine decides it within 2ⁿ steps; the technique says doublings stay doublings; P differs from machine to machine","A set is fast when some machine decides it within a power of n; the technique says two fast machines in a row may be slow; P is the same only for one-tape machines"],"why":"P is about a growth family, 39k’s powers, not about a machine."},
 {"u":"41h2.1","q":"What is a certificate, and when is a set in NP? What does the certificate-search technique say, and why is it slow?","a":"A set A is in NP when there are a fast checker C and a power p such that x is in A exactly when some string c no longer than p(|x|), the certificate, makes C say yes on x # c; every set in NP is decided within 2 to the power of a power of n steps, by running C on each of the at most 2^(p(n)+1) strings c: the count of certificates doubles with each letter","w":["A certificate is a proof that x is outside A; a set is in NP when it is not fast; the technique says NP sets are undecided","A certificate is any string; a set is in NP when some checker says yes on every c; the technique decides it within a power of n","A certificate is a short string the checker accepts; a set is in NP when it has one certificate for all inputs; the technique is slow because C is slow"],"why":"A row that makes a statement true is checked in one pass; every fast set is in NP, with the empty certificate."},
 {"u":"41h3.1","q":"What does it mean for a set to be NP-complete? What does the tableau technique say, and why is checking 2 × 3 windows enough to make the table a run?","a":"It is in NP and every set in NP fast-reduces to it; every set in NP fast-reduces to the satisfiable statements: the checker’s run within t steps is written as a table of t rows of t cells, with letters for “row i, cell j holds s”; by the one-next technique a cell is fixed by the three above it, so a finite list of allowed windows makes each row the next configuration of the row above","w":["It is the hardest set of all; the technique says satisfiability is fast; windows are checked because the table is too big to read","It is in NP and reduces to every set in NP; the technique says every statement is satisfiable; a window of 2 × 3 holds the whole configuration","It is in NP and not in P; the technique lists the satisfiable statements; windows are enough because each row has three cells"],"why":"If satisfiability is fast, every set in NP is fast."},
 {"u":"41h4.1","q":"What does the more-time technique say? Why does D pad its input with 0s, and which earlier argument does it repeat?","a":"For each k, some set is decided within a power of n steps and by no machine within nᵏ steps; U needs at most c_M nᵏ steps per run of M, and c_M nᵏ < n^(k+1) once n > c_M, so the padding 0ᵐ makes n large enough for D to see M’s answer and say the opposite; it is the diagonal of 41d2","w":["Every set decided at all is decided within nᵏ steps for some k; D pads to slow M down; it repeats the pumping technique","For each k some set is decided within nᵏ and by no machine within n^(k+1); D pads so that its input is a square; it repeats the tableau technique","More time decides nothing new; the padding is to fill the tape; it repeats 41c3’s count"],"why":"D beats M on one long enough input, and one is all it needs."},
 {"u":"41h5.1","q":"What is PSPACE, and why is every set in NP in it? What does the halving-room technique say, and why does it save room but not time?","a":"The sets decided in a power of n cells; try the certificates one at a time, reusing the same cells; if a list of N states has a fast test of which may follow which, whether u leads to v is decided in room about (log₂ N)² cells times the room of one state: a path of 2ᵏ steps has a middle state w, and the halves are checked one after the other in the same room; room can be reused and time cannot","w":["The sets decided within a power of n steps; NP is in it because certificates are short; the technique halves the time and the room","The sets decided in 2ⁿ cells; NP is in it only if P = NP; the technique checks the two halves side by side, so it saves time and not room","The sets decided in a power of n cells; NP is in it because all the certificates are stored at once; the technique saves room by forgetting u and v"],"why":"Each level keeps u, v, w, and there are log₂ N levels."},
 {"u":"41h6.1","q":"Why does a 2ⁿ method for satisfiability not show it is not fast? Why is every fast set in NP?","a":"One method takes 2ⁿ; whether every method does is open (41·now2); and NP means a yes has a short certificate checked fast, which a fast set has with the empty certificate","w":["A 2ⁿ method shows no faster one exists; and NP means not fast, so no fast set is in it","Satisfiability is proved to need 2ⁿ steps; and fast sets are in NP only when they are NP-complete","The 2ⁿ method is fast for small n; and every set is in NP"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41i"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41i"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41i1.1","q":"What is a coin machine, and when is a set in BPP? What does the majority-vote technique say, and which technique of rung 30 does its proof use?","a":"A tape machine with an extra tape of independent coin flips, each 0 or 1 with chance one half; A is in BPP when a coin machine runs within a power of n steps and on every input gives the wrong answer with chance at most 1/3; k independent runs, answered by the majority, are wrong with chance at most 8/k; the proof uses the spread-bound technique (30h), with the average and spread of 30g","w":["A machine that guesses its input; A is in BPP when the machine is right on most inputs; the majority of k runs is wrong with chance (1/3)ᵏ; the proof uses 30f only","A tape machine with coin flips; A is in BPP when it is right with chance above 0; the majority of k runs is never wrong; the proof uses pigeonhole","A tape machine with coin flips; A is in BPP when it is wrong with chance at most 1/3 on every input; the majority of k runs is wrong with chance at most k/8; the proof uses 30a"],"why":"The chance of error is stated before the coins are thrown, for every input alike."},
 {"u":"41i2.1","q":"What does the random-point technique say? How does its proof go from one letter to k letters, and which technique of rung 25 starts it?","a":"If a polynomial p in k letters has degree at most d and is not the zero polynomial, and each letter is drawn at random from a set of s numbers, p is 0 there with chance at most d/s; sort p by the power of x₁, with top coefficient q_j of degree at most d − j: by induction q_j is 0 with chance at most (d − j)/s, and otherwise p is a nonzero polynomial in x₁ of degree j, 0 with chance at most j/s; the root technique (25d) gives the case of one letter","w":["A nonzero polynomial is 0 at a random point with chance at most s/d; the proof multiplies the chances for each letter; it starts from 25a","A nonzero polynomial of degree d is never 0 at a random point; the proof tries every point; it starts from the root technique","A polynomial of degree d is 0 at a random point with chance at most d/s; the proof multiplies it out into its terms; it starts from 25e’s root choice"],"why":"(d − j)/s + j/s = d/s: the chance of an or is at most the sum (30f)."},
 {"u":"41i3.1","q":"What does the random-column technique say? Why can at most one of the two values of r_j make the ith entry of Dr zero?","a":"If AB ≠ C and r is a column of n entries, each 0 or 1 at random, then A(Br) = Cr with chance at most 1/2; with D = AB − C and d_ij ≠ 0, fix every other entry of r: the ith entry of Dr at r_j = 0 and at r_j = 1 differs by d_ij ≠ 0, so the two values cannot both be 0","w":["If AB = C then A(Br) = Cr with chance 1/2; both values of r_j make the entry zero when d_ij ≠ 0","If AB ≠ C then A(Br) ≠ Cr for every r; neither value of r_j makes the entry zero","If AB ≠ C then A(Br) = Cr with chance at most 1/n; one value of r_j always makes the entry zero"],"why":"Twenty columns miss a wrong C with chance at most 2⁻²⁰, for about 20 · 3n² steps."},
 {"u":"41i4.1","q":"What does the all-coins technique say, and why does trying every coin string fit in small room? Why do coins not let a machine decide the stopping set?","a":"Every set in BPP is in PSPACE; the machine reads at most nᵃ coins, so it is run on every string of nᵃ coins, one after another, reusing the room, with a counter of nᵃ + 1 digits for the yes answers; and running a coin machine on every coin string gives its answer without chance, slowly, so whatever coins decide a plain machine decides","w":["Every set in BPP is in P; every coin string is tried at once, side by side; coins let a machine decide the stopping set with chance 2/3","Every set in PSPACE is in BPP; the coin strings are stored on the tape together; coins do not help because flips are not independent","Every set in BPP is in PSPACE; the room is small because only one coin string is ever tried; coins decide the stopping set, but slowly"],"why":"P sits in BPP, which sits in PSPACE; what coins buy is speed."},
 {"u":"41i5.1","q":"Why does the fast-fall technique bound 2^W and not W itself, and which two techniques of rung 30 does its proof use? How many runs does it ask for to bring the error below one in a billion?","a":"The spread bound uses only the spread of the count; a growing function of the wrong runs W gives more: the stray-bound technique (30h1) on 2^W, which is never negative, with E 2^W a product of k averages by independent-averages-multiply (30g3), gives at most (2√2/3)ᵏ ≈ 0.943ᵏ; below 10⁻⁹ needs k = 352 runs","w":["2^W is easier to count than W; it uses the spread-bound technique twice; one in a billion needs eight billion runs","W itself may be negative; it uses 30f and 30g; one in a billion needs 235 runs","2^W is bounded because it is the largest function; it uses 30h1 alone; one in a billion needs 18 runs"],"why":"One in a million needs 235 runs, against eight million by the majority-vote bound."},
 {"u":"41i6.1","q":"Why do ten zero values at random points not prove that p is the zero polynomial? Why must BPP’s chance of error hold on every input?","a":"That is a stated chance of error, at most (d/s)¹⁰, not a proof; and BPP asks for 1/3 on every input: a chance over inputs is a different claim","w":["Ten zeros prove it once ten is above the degree; and the chance may be taken over most inputs","Random points are never roots; and BPP’s chance holds on every input because inputs are random","Ten zeros prove it for ten letters; and the chance is over the machines, not the coins"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41j"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41j"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41j1.1","q":"What does the trace-line technique say? In the run of the two-state machine, which row is used at step 3, and what does it change?","a":"To run a machine by hand, write one configuration a line, find the row of the table for the state and the mark after it, and write the next line by changing that one cell, moving the state one place and renaming it; at step 3 the line is A 1 1, so the row is A1 → 1LB: the cell keeps its 1, the state moves one place left and becomes B, giving B 0 1 1","w":["Write the whole tape once and cross cells off; at step 3 the row is B0 → 1LA, which writes a 1 and stops","Write one configuration a line; at step 3 the row is A0 → 1RB, which writes a 1 and steps right, giving 1 1 B 0","Write only the states, one a line; at step 3 the row is B1 → 1R stop, which ends the run"],"why":"The run stops after 6 steps with four 1s: 7·then’s BB(2) = 6."},
 {"u":"41j2.1","q":"What are the two ways of this footing to see that a machine never stops? Why does the blank-march technique need the machine never to step left of the first cell?","a":"A configuration seen twice comes round again for ever, by the back-again and one-next techniques; and the blank-march technique: in state q on a blank cell with only blanks to its right, and later the same further right, having never stepped left of the first cell; then between the two moments it read only cells that were blank at the first, so from the second moment it does what it did from the first, shifted right","w":["A run past 1000 steps never stops; and a tape that grows never stops; stepping left is forbidden because the tape has no left","A configuration seen twice; and a machine that writes only 1s; the march needs no condition on stepping left","A state seen twice never stops; and blank-march, which needs the machine to step left once so that it reads its own marks"],"why":"A0 → 1RB, B0 → 0RA marches: its tape reads 1010… and grows for ever."},
 {"u":"41j3.1","q":"What does the one-way technique say? Why does the machine’s next state depend on its state alone once it is past the input?","a":"If a machine with q states never steps left, then on an input of length n it stops within n + q steps or never stops; after n steps the head is past the input and reads only blanks, so the mark is always blank and the row is fixed by the state","w":["A machine that never steps left stops within n steps; past the input it has no next state","A machine that never steps left never stops; past the input its state depends on the marks it wrote","A machine with q states stops within q steps on every input; past the input it reads its input again"],"why":"Among the states at steps n, …, n + q two are the same, and from there they cycle."},
 {"u":"41j4.1","q":"What does the flip-diagonal technique say? On which input is the would-be decider D₀ wrong, and why does the answer not depend on the length of ⟨E⟩?","a":"A machine that, on the code of machine i, stops exactly when machine i does not stop on its own code differs from every machine of the table at that machine’s own column; D₀ is wrong on ⟨E⟩ # ⟨E⟩: if ⟨E⟩ is odd, D₀ says E does not stop, so E stops, and if even, D₀ says it stops, and E loops; either way E does the opposite of what D₀ says","w":["The flipped machine agrees with every row at its own column; D₀ is wrong on ⟨M₂⟩; the answer depends on the length of ⟨E⟩","The flipped machine differs from each row everywhere; D₀ is wrong on every input of odd length; the length of ⟨E⟩ decides which","The flipped machine is no machine; D₀ is wrong on no input; ⟨E⟩ has no length"],"why":"For the four machines the diagonal is stops, never, never, stops, and E flips each entry."},
 {"u":"41j5.1","q":"Why do 1000 steps with no repeat not show that a machine never stops? Why does the table of four machines not show that E is no machine?","a":"It may stop at step 1001: a catch needs a repeat, a march or a proof; and the table shows only that E is none of these four: E is a machine, and its row is new","w":["1000 steps are more than any stopping machine takes; and the table shows E cannot be built","A machine with no repeat in 1000 steps has a growing tape, so it never stops; and E is one of the four","Only 5 steps are ever needed; and the table has no room for a fifth row"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41k"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41k"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41k1.1","q":"What is a clause, and what does the clause-split technique say? In its proof, how is the new letter z chosen in each case?","a":"A clause is an or of letters and nots of letters; a ∨ b ∨ c ∨ d has a true row exactly when (a ∨ b ∨ z) ∧ (¬z ∨ c ∨ d) does, for a new letter z; when a ∨ b is true put z false, and when c ∨ d is true put z true","w":["A clause is an and of letters; a ∨ b ∨ c ∨ d has a true row exactly when (a ∨ b ∨ z) ∧ (z ∨ c ∨ d) does; z is always put true","A clause is an or of letters and nots of letters; the split has a true row for every a, b, c, d; z is chosen by a coin","A clause is an or of exactly three letters; the split needs no new letter; z is one of a, b, c, d"],"why":"A clause with k letters becomes k − 2 clauses of three, with k − 3 new letters."},
 {"u":"41k2.1","q":"What is a three-colouring of a map? What does the palette technique say, and how does a colouring give a row of a table?","a":"It gives each dot one of three colours so that no link joins two dots of one colour; in a three-colouring of a triangle T, F, B, with x and x̄ linked to each other and both to B, the dots x and x̄ take the colours of T and F, one each; x is true in the row when its dot is coloured like T","w":["It gives each link one of three colours; the palette technique says x and x̄ take the same colour; a row is read from the colour of B","It gives each dot a colour so that linked dots agree; x and x̄ are both spare; a row is read from the links","It gives each dot one of three colours so that linked dots differ; x and x̄ may take any two colours; x is true when its dot is spare"],"why":"Neither is spare, since both are linked to B, and they differ."},
 {"u":"41k3.1","q":"What does the or-piece technique say? Why is o forced false when both inputs are false, and how does a clause of three use two or-pieces?","a":"If a and b are both coloured false, o is coloured false; if either is coloured true, the colouring can be finished with o true; with both false, u and v are not false and differ, so they are true and spare, and o, linked to both, has only false left; a clause a ∨ b ∨ c takes o₁ from a and b, then o₂ from o₁ and c, with o₂ linked to F and to B so that it must be true","w":["If either input is true, o is forced true; with both false o may be any colour; a clause of three uses one piece for each letter","If both inputs are false, o is spare; o is forced by its link to F; a clause uses two pieces side by side, both read from a","If both inputs are true, o is false; with both false, u and v are both spare; a clause of three links o₁ to T"],"why":"Some finishing gives o true, and that is all the piece needs."},
 {"u":"41k4.1","q":"What does the map-from-clauses technique say, and how many dots does the map of a statement with n letters and m clauses have? How is a true row read off a three-colouring?","a":"A statement with n letters and m clauses of three has a true row exactly when its map has a three-colouring; the map has 3 + 2n + 6m dots and 3 + 3n + 12m links; the row is read off the pairs, x true when its dot is coloured like T, and it makes every clause true, since an all-false clause would force o₂ false, against its links","w":["A statement has a true row exactly when its map has two colours; the map has n + m dots; the row is read off the triangle","A statement with a true row has a map with no three-colouring; the map has 3ⁿ dots; the row is read off the clause pieces","A statement has a true row exactly when its map has a three-colouring; the map has 3 + n + 3m dots; the row is read by trying all colourings"],"why":"Three letters and three clauses give 27 dots and 48 links."},
 {"u":"41k5.1","q":"Why must the second clause of a split hold ¬z and not z? Why is the reduction fast although the map has 3²⁷ ways to colour?","a":"With z in both, z true makes both clauses hold whatever a, b, c, d are; and the reduction writes the map, in one pass along the statement, and never tries the colourings","w":["z and ¬z are the same letter, so either will do; and the reduction tries only the first 27 colourings","¬z makes the clauses shorter; and the reduction is slow, which is why colouring is hard","The second clause needs ¬z so that z is false in every row; and 3²⁷ is a power of n"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
(window.LADDER_CARDS["41l"]=window.LADDER_CARDS["41l"]||[]).push(
 {"u":"41l1.1","q":"When are two maps the same shape? What does the which-one technique say, and why does a prover facing two maps of the same shape guess right only half the time?","a":"When a matching of their dots (9c) carries links to links and non-links to non-links; if two maps are not the same shape, a prover names which of them the checker shuffled every time, and if they are, any prover names it with chance exactly one half, so k rounds fool the checker with chance at most 2⁻ᵏ; a random shuffle of either map gives every map of that shape alike, so what the prover sees is independent of the checker’s coin","w":["When they have the same number of dots; a prover names the shuffled map with chance one half in both cases; the prover is slow","When a matching carries links to links; if the maps are the same shape the prover is always right; with different shapes it guesses","When they have the same links; k rounds fool the checker with chance 1/k; the prover is right half the time because it sees only half the map"],"why":"Twenty rounds bring the chance of being fooled below one in a million."},
 {"u":"41l2.1","q":"What does a locked box do, and what is held in trust about it? What does the locked-colours technique say, and why must the boxes be locked before the checker picks a link?","a":"Once sent its content cannot be changed, and the checker cannot see it until the prover sends the key; that such boxes can be built, from functions fast to compute and believed slow to undo, is held in trust until rung 43; if the map has no three-colouring each round catches the prover with chance at least 1/l, so k · l rounds fool the checker with chance at most e⁻ᵏ; locked first, the colours are fixed before the pick, so some link has its ends alike","w":["A locked box hides the map; nothing is held in trust; each round catches a cheat with chance 1/2; the order of locking and picking does not matter","A locked box can be reopened and changed by the prover; rung 43 is held in trust; a cheat is caught in every round; the boxes are locked after the pick","A locked box holds a colour; held in trust is that the checker is honest; k rounds fool the checker with chance 1/l; locking first lets the prover rename the colours"],"why":"(1 − 1/l)^(kl) ≤ e⁻ᵏ, since ln(1 − 1/l) ≤ −1/l (17)."},
 {"u":"41l3.1","q":"What does the self-made-record technique say? Why is each of the 6 ordered pairs equally likely, and why can the checker not use the record to convince anyone else?","a":"The record of each round, the link and the two colours shown, has the same chances as a record the checker could make alone, with its own coins and no colouring; a random renaming of the three colours sends the two different colours at the link’s ends to each of the 6 ordered pairs with chance 1/6; so the checker could have made the record up, and it is no evidence to anyone else","w":["The record shows the whole colouring after l rounds; the pairs are equally likely because the map is random; the record convinces anyone who sees it","The record has the chances of a true colouring only; each pair has chance 1/3; the checker cannot share it because the boxes stay locked","The record is empty; no pair is shown; the checker has nothing to share"],"why":"The checker gains certainty and no information: a zero-knowledge proof."},
 {"u":"41l4.1","q":"What does the through-colouring technique say? Which techniques of 41h and 41k does it pass through, and what does the prover need to know?","a":"With locked boxes, every set in NP has a proof of this kind: a liar is caught at a known rate, and the checker learns nothing it could not make alone; the tableau technique (41h3) and the map-from-clauses technique (41k4) turn x into a map G_x, and the prover, knowing a certificate, has a true row and so a three-colouring of G_x for the locked-colours rounds","w":["Every decided set has such a proof; it passes through the pumping and clause-split techniques; the prover needs to know nothing","Every set in NP has a proof of this kind without locked boxes; it passes through 41h4 and 41k1; the prover needs the checker’s coins","Only three-colouring has such a proof; it passes through 41k3 alone; the prover needs every colouring of the map"],"why":"So a person can prove they know a password or a factor without giving it away."},
 {"u":"41l5.1","q":"Why must the colours be renamed afresh each round? Why is the checker’s record no evidence for a judge?","a":"With the same renaming the rounds together show the whole colouring, link by link; and the checker could have made the record alone, so it is no evidence to anyone else","w":["Renaming is only to keep the prover busy; and the record is full evidence, since every answer was true","A fresh renaming makes cheating easier to catch; and the judge cannot read locked boxes","The colours wear out after one round; and the record is evidence only after l rounds"],"why":"Both are rows of the step’s Moves to Watch Out For."});
/* end phase F und_r41 */
